INT. MARS COLONY MESS TENT - EVENING

Plastic tables. Sand drifting under the flap. A lamp swings from the ridgepole.

CAPTAIN BRYONY ASHWORTH (40s, sunburnt) deals protein bars out of a crate like cards. ENGINEER TAVITA FETU (30s, broad) leans against the pole. BOTANIST LAILA NASSIR (30s, thin, notes) writes in a ledger.

ASHWORTH
Two each. Tavita, you have three coming.

TAVITA
I'll take two.

ASHWORTH
You'll take three. You haul the panels.

TAVITA
Others haul too.

LAILA
(not looking up)
Everyone hauls. It's tent rules.

ASHWORTH
It's my rule. I'm Captain.

LAILA
Then Captain, the ledger says we have eleven days of rations left.

Bryony stops dealing. Tavita pushes off the pole.

TAVITA
You said twenty.

LAILA
Twenty was the count before the dust storm took crate six.

ASHWORTH
Crate six was mine.

LAILA
I noticed.

ASHWORTH
Say what you mean.

LAILA
I mean you dealt me two bars and kept five.

Ashworth looks at the bars in her hand. She does not deny it.

TAVITA
Bry.

ASHWORTH
They're not for me.

LAILA
Then who?

Tavita steps in and takes the crate. Laila stands. Ashworth grabs the crate's other handle. They all pull, and the crate tips, bars scattering across the sand-dusted floor.

They freeze. Nobody bends to gather them.

ASHWORTH (CONT'D)
(quietly)
They're for Tavita's kid. Back on the Hab. The ration cut starts Monday.

Tavita lets go.
